00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.940
This is the AI briefing for Wednesday, November 11.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:25.000
I'm Michael.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Like everything here, this briefing is written and read by AI from public sources.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.640
Today is about money, chips, prices, and rules.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:40.460
Anthropic has filed to raise up to $88.5 billion through a public listing and a private placement,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.660
with NVIDIA among the backers.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:48.320
NVIDIA's own board is opening an independent investigation into Jensen H. Wong,

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.100
and that may matter more for export controls than for governance.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:01:00.000
Google DeepMind has opened Gemini 4 Carbon to everyone at $2 and $10 per million tokens.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.380
That price is set to double on the 1st of December,

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:08.800
and New York is enforcing an AI safety law on OpenAI and Anthropic,

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.340
with incident reporting and fines.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.920
The main story is first, then a few quick hits.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:21.080
First story, an update on Anthropic's listing.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:27.100
The S1 is now out, and Crunchbase News reports it seeks up to $88.5 billion.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.540
That means 801 million new shares.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:37.080
They're priced at $70 to $78, alongside a $26 billion private placement.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:44.500
The filing shows 9-month revenue of $33.2 billion against $2.5 billion.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:52.700
TechMeme reports NVIDIA plans, a $10 billion investment after the IPO other reports,

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:58.440
say the founder's plan equal exits of $250 million each at the midpoint.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:05.680
The filing terms check out. What nothing yet explains is a $104 billion net loss

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.580
sitting beside an operating loss of $1.3 billion.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:16.580
dollars. Our read holds. The revenue looks real and the financing looks partly circular,

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:23.060
since NVIDIA's money likely flows back into its chips. We lean bare on the financing, not the

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:32.720
business. Staying with NVIDIA, the second story is also an update. Ars Technica reports the board

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:38.480
will run an independent investigation into Chief Executive Jensen H. Wong, under pressure from

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:45.280
Congress. Ed Zitron's commentary adds the detail. Huang has admitted an affair with Alice Huang,

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:51.720
who runs Megaspeed. Commerce has probed Megaspeed for smuggling since late the year before last,

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:59.420
according to reporting Zitron cites. Short-seller Culper cites a $4.8 billion figure for Speed

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:05.180
Matrix, calling it the largest Southeast Asian customer. The congressional pressure and the

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:11.780
board probe are established facts. The affair details trace to a memo. Megaspeed's role as a

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:17.040
major customer, though, is inferred from the short seller's claims and culprit profits if

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.600
NVIDIA's stock falls. We see an export control story before a governance one.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:29.660
If Megaspeed was a major channel, enforcement on Southeast Asian compute likely titans.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:36.640
For startups buying GPS through resellers there, a fair question is where their compute physically sits.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.840
Third story and a shift from financing to pricing.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.920
Google DeepMind has opened Gemini 4 Carbon to all users.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:49.700
Until now it was reserved for cyber defenders.

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:55.480
The launch price is $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output,

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.380
according to Google DeepMind and Simon Willison.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:02.800
On the 1st of December, that rises to $4 and $20.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.760
The capability claims are bolder.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:10.100
Demi's Hassabis says it reverse engineered theme park binaries.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:18.980
Perplexity says it is 2.5 times cheaper than Fable 5.2 for agentic research, without stating how it priced that.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.820
Ethan Mollick, who had early access, called it very good.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:28.280
With different strengths from Claude and GPT, he used it to reproduce a video in 3JS.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:32.840
So how well does this hold up? The rollout and the pricing are confirmed.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:36.260
The capability claims rest on internal benchmarks,

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:41.000
unverified DEMOS and cost comparisons with no stated basis.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.880
Nobody has independently validated performance or cost.

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:50.120
Willison's own hands-on test found it handles SVGs,

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:54.940
but he saw no new capability. Our read is that this is a pricing event.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:05:01.020
A launch price that doubles within weeks looks like an introductory discount, not a new floor.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:06.080
That matters for agent startups that built their unit economics on the launch rate.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:14.900
On the 1st of December, their costs jumped two times, and per-task margins modeled at $2 and $10 may not survive.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:18.460
We'd want to see what those margins look like at the higher price.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:21.120
The AGI forward reading is different.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.840
Google held carbon back for cyber defenders before releasing it widely

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:33.240
Taken with the theme park claim that points to a capability jump in security work, not only a price cut

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:40.200
The fourth and last of the main stories moves from markets to regulation

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:47.460
Yesterday, TechCrunch reported that New York is enforcing its AI safety law on OpenAI and Anthropic

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:54.140
The law requires them to register, to disclose their safety practices, and to report critical incidents.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:57.320
Companies that don't comply face potential fines.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:00.280
The reporting on how enforcement works is solid.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.820
Two details narrow its reach.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:06.920
The real obligations start on the 1st of January next year.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.580
And they apply only to developers with more than $500 million in revenue.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:17.520
In our view, this partly cuts against what we've called the security reckoning view,

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:21.460
because New York set incident reporting before any major disaster.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.440
It does so only for the largest developers, though.

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:30.360
We also think it opens a market for tooling that logs incidents and handles safety disclosures.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:36.700
SEED and Series A model builders sit far below that $500 million threshold.

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:42.760
But selling to the covered labs could bring incident reporting clauses into their contracts.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:47.800
A question worth asking might be whether a startup's contracts with big labs already

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:54.400
carry those terms, and who carries the liability. From a policy angle, the reading is more favorable.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:07:01.060
The threshold spares startups while binding the labs that matter, and a state law with fines and

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:10.420
incident reporting may become the template other states copy two quick hits before the close open

